      <body>
         <entry xml:id="hedyokhin-dlgenr"
            xml:lang="en"
            type="mainEntry"
            prev="#"
            next="#"
            source="Genesis Rabbah">
            <form type="lemma" opt="false">
               <orth type="lemma" xml:lang="jpa" opt="false" select="true">הדיוכין</orth>
               <orth type="transliteration" xml:lang="fjb" opt="false">hdywkyn</orth>
               <pron notation="rabbinic" opt="false">hedyokhin</pron>
               <gramGrp>
                  <gram type="pos">noun</gram>
                  <gram type="gen">m.</gram>
                  <gram type="num">pl.</gram>
               </gramGrp>
            </form>
            <sense xml:id="hedyokhin-dlgenr-1">
               <cit type="translation" xml:lang="en" subtype="sense">
                  <quote>charioteers [in circus]</quote>
               </cit>
               <cit type="translationEquivalent" xml:lang="grc">
                  <quote>ἡνίοχος</quote>
               </cit>
               <cit type="example" xml:lang="jpa">
                  <quote>למלך שנכנס למדינה וקלסו אותו בני המדינה וערב לו קילוסן הרבה להם בריצה והרבה להם 
                     <app>
                        <lem>בהדיוכים</lem>
                        <rdg wit="#V">בהדיוכין</rdg>
                        <rdg wit="#K">בדיוכין</rdg>
                     </app>, לאחר זמן הכעיסוהו ומיעט להם בריצה ומיעט להם 
                     <app>
                        <lem>בהדיוכים</lem>
                        <rdg wit="#K">בדיוכין</rdg>
                     </app></quote>
                  <bibl>
                     <ref type="witness">10,4</ref>
                     <ref type="witness">I 76: 3</ref>
                  </bibl>
                  <cit type="translation" xml:lang="en">
                     <quote>This may be compared to a king who entered a province, and its inhabitants praised him, which pleased him; thereupon he entertained them with many races and charioteers. Subsequently they angered him, whereupon he reduced the race meetings and the charioteers. (Freedman / Simon I 72)</quote>
                  </cit>
                  <note>= <bibl type="ancient">Yalq 17 [5c: 5]</bibl></note>
               </cit>
            </sense>
            <note>Sg. not attested. Cf. <bibl type="ancient">MidrPs 17,5 (64b)</bibl>: <term xml:lang="jpa">בנוהג שבעולם הנדייסין רצין באיפרכוס</term> (= <bibl type="ancient">Yalq Ps 670 [447b: 9]</bibl> (<term xml:lang="jpa">הנדיוסים</term>)). Krauss renders: <term xml:lang="jpa">הנדיוכין</term> <term xml:lang="fjb">hndywkhyn</term> or <term xml:lang="jpa">הנדיוכסין</term> <term xml:lang="fjb">hndywkhsyn</term>. <term xml:lang="grc">ἡενίοχος</term> was already suggested by <bibl type="modern">Perles (1872) 137-138</bibl>. See also <bibl type="modern">AC III 184ab</bibl>. <bibl type="modern">Jastrow I 295b</bibl> (and <bibl type="modern">Dalman 92a</bibl>) read <term xml:lang="jpa">דידכאות</term> <term xml:lang="fjb">dydkhʾwt</term> Gr. <term xml:lang="grc">διαδοχή</term> “relays”, “guards at stations“. The explanation from Gr. <term xml:lang="grc">ἡεδονή</term> (gen. pl. <term xml:lang="grc">ἡεδονῶν</term>) must be rejected (see <bibl type="modern">Brüll, FW 192</bibl>, Löw in Krauss ad loc. and Levy). <bibl type="modern">Brüll, FW 193</bibl> suggested Gr. <term xml:lang="grc">ἡενιοχεία</term>, rejected by <bibl type="modern">Levy I 453b</bibl>, who suggested sem. etymology (but see Fleischer in Levy I 557a).</note>
            <ref type="bibliography">
               <bibl>
                  <title>Krauss, LW II</title>
                  <citedRange>231a</citedRange>
               </bibl>
            </ref>
         </entry>
